cal prisoner, so he was afraid of the Red Terror and hid in the northern mountains. No one knew about it, not even the Inagaki family, except for a few people.\" [2036] \"Is he staying in this house?\" [2037] \"Yes, on the third floor, across from your room, Murakami-san.\" [2038] This was during the pre-war period when the Kuj\u014d residence was still used as the main house. [2039] \"Is this the song he composed while staying here?\" [2040] \"He dedicated this piece to my grandfather. The song is called 'Imakagami.' This record was made thirty years ago by a record company. At the time, Medvedev's name was omitted, and it was claimed to be composed by my grandfather.\" [2041] Looking up, I saw that the record sleeve indeed read \"Composed by Tomomichi Imai.\" [2042] \"However, this song is quite dark. It seems to require a high level of technique from the cello soloist.\" [2043] \"Isn't that true for an artist who has lost his country?\" [2044] Kanai sighed and lowered his eyes again. The mournful wails of the first and second violins merged together. [2045] For a moment, we stopped talking and listened to this requiem arranged in a quintet. [2046] Was Medvedev in Japan, reminiscing about St. Petersburg or mourning the Romanov dynasty that fell into tragedy? [2047] \"This piece became his final work,\" Kanayama suddenly said. \"One reason he didn't leave any other works after his escape was that he could only create this one piece. Medvedev passed away six months after arriving in Kurosawa City.\" [2048] \"Did he die of disappointment?\" [2049] \"No\u2026\" Kanayama sadly shook his head. \"He was over fifty at the time, so his body was already weak\u2026 However, he drowned, and his body was found in the pond behind the house.\" [2050] \"Drowned\u2026 Was he murdered?\" [2051] Mutsu's words were cautious, but he was clearly shocked. [2052] \"I don't know. Maybe he just drowned, but the police believe it was the work of a communist. Of course, the police just wanted an excuse to suppress a thinker.\" [2053] \"Mysterious indeed,\" Mutsu murmured like a romanticist. [2054] \"By the way, why did Medvedev come to the Mochizuki family?\" [2055] \"Who knows? My grandfather and great-grandfather served in Manchuria, so maybe it was some noble who fled at the time who introduced them.\" [2056] Mutsumi wanted to probe further, but Kaname interrupted him: \"Mr. Mutsumi, you didn't come here to listen to me explain this record, did you?\" [2057] He smiled, but it was clear that he was wary, and his attitude was very different from when he had asked Mutsumi to investigate the fog painting. [2058] \"Not much different. I just came to chat.\" [2059] \"Chat?\" Kaname was not going to take Mutsumi's words at face value. \"A murder case also becomes a topic of conversation?\" [2060] \"Yes,\" Mutsumi lowered his tone calmly, \"I feel bad that Mr. Iwaya passed away so recently.\" [2061] \"It's okay. I know I'm a suspect too.\" Sugiyama's face broke into a weak smile. [2062] \"How much is the inheritance in total?\" [2063] Sugiyama hesitated for a moment before answering, \"Uh, roughly around 500 billion yen. I think asking a lawyer would give you the exact figure.\" [2064] \"500 billion yen? That's hard to imagine.\" [2065] Could Mutsuji think the motive was the inheritance? Nowadays, even a murder case for a mere 10,000 yen is possible, but 500 billion yen would make a good show of a struggle for the inheritance both valuable and meaningful. [2066] \"Saying 500 billion, it's actually more than half land and stocks, and most of them are in the Northern Mountain. If we don't sell them, we may not even be able to pay the inheritance tax. The rest are almost entirely Mirai Group stocks, with only about 10% actually owned.\" [2067] Nevertheless, it would allow us to immediately mobilize tens of billions of yen.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03