f his chair. [803] As for what the police gained from this fruitless dialogue, it was the testimony of the housekeeper, Yoshiko. [804] Yoshiko gave two important testimonies. One was about a letter. Last night, when she delivered dinner to Yoshinobu's room, she saw him writing on a note, which could easily be the letter to the detective in the town of Higashiyama. [805] The Inspector read the letter, which was similar to the one sent to the Inspector but did not contain any details about the case. However, it was quite peculiar that Yoshinobu wanted to hire another detective after the Inspector had decided to come to the Kurogane Castle. [806] The other one - actually more significant - further aggravated the detective's disappointment. It was related to the key of the \"Hell Gate\". [807] As previously mentioned, the room with the horse corpse, the \"Hell Gate\", is located in the tower, surrounded by thick stone walls on all sides except for the door. There are no windows, only several arrow holes about 8 meters high. [808] When the body was found, the door with the hell pattern was locked, and the \"Hell Gate\" was completely sealed. There are two keys to the door, one original and one spare. The original key was held in the hand of the corpse of the horse. Initially, everyone thought that the spare key, which was kept by Yasu, was used to lock the \"Hell Gate\", but this view was rejected through this record. [809] \"The spare key and other spare keys were kept in a wall-mounted safe, and it has not been used for several years. Until I opened the safe at the request of the detective, the surface of the safe was covered with a uniform layer of thin dust. If someone had opened it recently, there would have been fingerprints.\" [810] The housekeeper spoke confidently. No matter how the detective tried to question her, the answer remained the same. Moreover, the more she repeated the question, the more convinced she became. [811] \"I won,\" said Kumotani, but he did