d from the outside. That place was like a stone prison. There are many ways to do it.\" [829] \"Many ways, such as?\" [830] \"Charm, telekinesis... But I think you understand it won't be those clich\u00e9d methods.\" [831] \"Is it about pride?\" [832] \"These days, lines and ropes are no longer fashionable.\" [833] \"This kind of guy should have been eliminated earlier!\" [834] \"Don't get too impatient. We still have unexplored areas.\" [835] Regarding the locked room, Kumotsubo didn't seem to have any specific ideas. However, his calm demeanor suggested that he might have found a way to approach the problem. [836] \"By the way, Detective Jotaro,\" Kumagai changed the subject, \"Have you heard of 'Love of Three Oranges'?\" [837] \"I don't know... Detective Kodama, do you know?\" [838] Detective Kodama nodded, a bit reserved: \"It's a Prokofiev opera. I remember it was later arranged into a song cycle.\" [839] \"Love of Three Oranges is a masterpiece written by Soviet composer Prokofiev during his escape. He was not yet thirty years old at the time, and this work, along with 'Lieutenant Kije,' has become his signature piece.\" [840] \"I've only heard of 'Peter and the Wolf.'\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."